(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-1-pregnancy-breastfeeding-quiz"]={"title":"Pregnancy & Breastfeeding — CAM/Nutrition Exam 1","category":"CAM / Nutrition","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which statement best reflects how the lecture frames the relationship between maternal nutrition and pregnancy outcomes?","choices":["Maternal nutritional status is a modifiable risk factor, and maintaining a healthy weight and good nutrition before and during pregnancy can optimize pregnancy outcomes.","Maternal nutritional status has essentially little influence on pregnancy outcomes once conception has already occurred.","Reproductive hormone synthesis and normal menstrual cycling require only a minimal caloric input when compared with pregnancy itself.","Nutritional optimization is only considered relevant during breastfeeding, not at any point prior to conception."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. This directly matches the lecture's framing of reproductive function and nutrition. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A woman with pregestational diabetes has poorly controlled hyperglycemia during weeks 3-8 of embryonic development. Why does the lecture flag this window as especially concerning?","choices":["This is when the fetus doubles in length and increases most in weight, matching the lecture's own description of the third-trimester fetal growth patterns.","This is the period of organogenesis, when major organs are forming, and maternal hyperglycemia during this window is associated with an increased risk of congenital malformations.","This is the period when iron intake takes priority for the developing fetus, according to the lecture's description of fetal needs.","This is when pregnancy weight gain should be at its fastest pace, roughly 1 pound gained per week, according to the lecture."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states organogenesis occurs during weeks 3-8, and maternal hyperglycemia during this critical period is associated with increased risk of congenital malformations because excess glucose crosses the placenta and can disrupt embryonic development. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed in the lecture as a consequence of maternal obesity during the preconception and pregnancy period?","choices":["Increased fertility and likelihood of spontaneous conception.","Decreased risk of cesarean delivery.","Increased risk of hypertensive disorders of pregnancy.","Decreased postpartum weight retention."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture explicitly lists increased risk of hypertensive disorders of pregnancy among the preconception/pregnancy effects of obesity. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A patient had a prior pregnancy affected by a neural tube defect and is now planning another pregnancy. What preconception folic acid dose does the lecture indicate for her?","choices":["0.4 mg/day, the same as a woman with no history of a neural tube defect.","0.6 mg/day, which is the dose recommended during an ongoing pregnancy itself.","220 mcg/day, a figure that actually matches the iodine RDA instead.","4 mg/day, given her history of a previous pregnancy affected by a neural tube defect."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture specifies 4 mg/day of folic acid preconception for women with a history of a previous pregnancy affected by a neural tube defect. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"How does the lecture define infertility for a 37-year-old woman attempting to conceive?","choices":["Inability to conceive after 6 months of unprotected intercourse, since she is 35 or older.","Inability to conceive after 1 year of unprotected intercourse, the same threshold used regardless of age.","Inability to conceive after 2 years of unprotected intercourse.","Inability to conceive despite a normal BMI and no other risk factors."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines infertility as inability to conceive after 6 months of unprotected intercourse for women 35 years or older, and this patient is 37. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"According to the lecture, how does excess body fat affect female fertility?","choices":["It lowers estrogen (estradiol) production, much as very low body fat does.","It increases estrogen levels, and infertility risk is threefold higher in obese than non-obese women.","It has no measurable effect at all on estrogen levels or conception rates.","It decreases sperm count, an effect seen only in males with excess body fat."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states excess body fat increases estrogen levels and that infertility risk is threefold higher in obese versus non-obese women. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A pregnant patient has severe dietary restrictions. Which fetal risk is specifically associated with maternal dietary restriction, as distinguished from the risks of maternal obesity in the lecture?","choices":["Macrosomia","Childhood obesity","Low fetal birth weight","Fetal neural tube defects"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states maternal dietary restrictions increase risk of poor fetal development and low fetal birth weight. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A pregnant patient reports craving and regularly eating clay and ice. Which statement about this behavior is accurate per the lecture?","choices":["This is a typical food craving, and the lecture states cravings in general are linked to specific nutritional deficiencies.","This is simply a food aversion, which the lecture treats as a normal, low-concern finding regardless of the quantity consumed.","This behavior is described in the lecture as being entirely unrelated to the patient's iron status.","This is pica, craving of non-food substances, which is concerning when consumed in large amounts and may be associated with iron deficiency."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines pica as craving non-food substances (examples include clay, starch, ice), notes it is concerning if consumed in large amounts, and states it may be associated with iron deficiency. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Why does the lecture identify the first trimester as the most critical period for potential fetal problems?","choices":["It is a finite window during which cells develop into tissues and organs, making the quality of nutrition especially important.","It is when the fetus doubles in length and gains the greatest amount of weight overall.","It is the period when the fetus can still be affected by toxins, though not as easily as in the later trimesters.","It is the period when iron intake specifically takes priority for the developing fetus."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes the first trimester as the most critical period for potential problems because it is a finite window of opportunity for cells to develop into tissue/organ, making nutritional quality most important. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Adults exposed to the Dutch Famine specifically during the first trimester of their mother's pregnancy showed increased risk of which condition, per the lecture's table of famine exposure outcomes?","choices":["Obstructive lung disease","Coronary heart disease","Kidney disease","Glucose intolerance only, with no risks unique to any specific trimester."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Coronary heart disease is listed specifically under first-trimester famine exposure in the lecture's table. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A newborn's fetal weight is documented at the 5th percentile for gestational age. What term does the lecture use for this classification?","choices":["Classified as macrosomic","Termed low birth weight","Small for gestational age (SGA)","Considered a post-term birth"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines SGA as fetal weight less than the 10th percentile for gestational age, which matches a fetus at the 5th percentile. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the recommended protein intake during pregnancy according to the lecture?","choices":["About 175 grams per day, representing roughly 50% of total daily calories.","Routine protein supplementation on top of a normal diet.","Between 20 and 35% of total daily calories should come from protein.","1.1 g/kg/day, or about 71 g/day, roughly 20% of daily total calories."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives 1.1 g/kg/day (about 71 g/day), roughly 20% of total daily calories, as the pregnancy protein recommendation. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is the RDA for iron during pregnancy, and why is it necessary according to the lecture?","choices":["27 mg/day, necessary for fetal and placental development and maternal red blood cell expansion.","11 mg/day, needed specifically to help prevent fetal growth restriction.","220 mcg/day, necessary specifically to help prevent fetal goiter formation.","600 IU/day, necessary specifically to support proper calcium absorption."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives 27 mg/day as the iron RDA in pregnancy, necessary for fetal/placental development and maternal RBC expansion. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What folic acid dose does ACOG recommend during an ongoing pregnancy for a woman without a history of a prior neural tube defect-affected pregnancy?","choices":["4 mg/day","0.6 mg/day","0.4 mg/day","450 mg/day"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states ACOG recommends 0.6 mg of folic acid during pregnancy. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which benefit of omega-3 fatty acid intake during pregnancy is supported in the lecture?","choices":["Consistently demonstrated, well-established long-term neurocognitive benefits for the developing child.","A meaningfully reduced risk of fetal goiter developing during gestation.","Support of normal fetal brain and eye development, and association with a lower risk of preterm birth.","Direct prevention of neural tube defects during early fetal development."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states omega-3 fatty acids support normal fetal brain and eye development and are associated with a lower risk of preterm birth. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Why does the lecture advise pregnant patients to avoid eating liver and to avoid taking excess prenatal vitamins, especially in the first trimester?","choices":["Liver is a major source of saturated fat, which should be limited to under 10% of total daily calories.","Liver can be a source of foodborne illness such as listeria if it is undercooked.","Liver is very high in iodine content, which can cause fetal goiter in excess.","Excessive vitamin A intake increases the risk of a teratogenic effect, and liver is a concentrated source of vitamin A."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states excessive vitamin A intake increases teratogenic risk and specifically advises avoiding liver, especially in the first trimester, for this reason. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A patient eats whole grains, vegetables, fruits, legumes, and seaweed, and limits animal foods to white meat or white-meat fish once or twice a week. Which diet type does this describe?","choices":["Macrobiotic","Pescatarian","Semi-vegetarian","Lacto-ovovegetarian"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines the macrobiotic diet as whole grains, vegetables, fruits, legumes, and seaweeds, with animal foods limited to white meat or white-meat fish once or twice a week. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A patient in her third trimester asks how many additional calories per day she needs above her prepregnancy intake. Based on the lecture, what should she be told?","choices":["No additional calories are needed; this reflects the myth of 'eating for two.'","About 450 additional kcal/day, and this still isn't the equivalent of literally 'eating for two.'","About 340 additional kcal per day above her prepregnancy intake.","About 500 additional kcal/day, exactly the same increase recommended during breastfeeding."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture specifies +450 kcal/day for the third trimester, while clarifying this is not the nutritional equivalent of 'eating for two.' — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What weight gain pattern does the lecture describe for a typical pregnancy?","choices":["About 1 pound per week throughout the whole of pregnancy, including the entire first trimester.","About 5 to 10 pounds total gained in the first trimester, then minimal gain afterward.","1-5 pounds total in the first trimester, then approximately 1 pound per week in the second and third trimesters.","No specific first-trimester target; weight gain begins only in the second trimester."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This matches the lecture's stated weight gain pattern. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which fish should a pregnant patient be counseled to avoid due to mercury content, per the lecture's guidance?","choices":["Wild salmon","Light tuna","Canned sardines","King mackerel"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists king mackerel among the high-mercury fish to avoid, along with shark, swordfish, marlin, orange roughy, tilefish, and bigeye tuna. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A pregnant patient reports nausea and vomiting. Which dietary/supplement strategy does the lecture recommend specifically for this discomfort?","choices":["Pyridoxine (vitamin B6), with or without doxylamine, ginger, and eating cold foods sparingly but often.","Increasing both the fluid intake and the dietary fibre consumption throughout the day.","Avoiding acidic foods and not eating close to bedtime each night.","Avoiding caffeine entirely, limiting intake to under 200 mg per day."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists pyridoxine with or without doxylamine, ginger, avoiding strong smells/spicy foods, cold foods, and eating sparingly but often as remedies for nausea and vomiting. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which hormone is responsible for the 'let-down reflex,' or milk ejection/release, during breastfeeding?","choices":["Prolactin","Oxytocin","Estrogen","Progesterone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states breastmilk ejection or release is promoted by oxytocin, described as the let-down reflex. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A mother is 2 days postpartum. Which characteristics would be expected of her breastmilk at this point, per the lecture?","choices":["Thick, yellowish colostrum, rich in immunoglobulins and immune cells, with a laxative effect that aids passage of meconium.","Mature milk that is about 87% water and fully established as the primary milk supply.","Transitional milk that is far more voluminous than colostrum, typically seen 2-14 days after the birth.","Milk in which hindmilk predominates throughout, resembling cream in consistency."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes colostrum, produced during the first few days after birth, as thick and yellowish, rich in immunoglobulins and immune cells, and having a laxative effect that aids passage of newborn meconium. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which immunoglobulin is most abundant in human breastmilk, and approximately what percentage of mature milk is water, per the lecture?","choices":["IgG; approximately 50% water","IgA; approximately 87% water","IgM; approximately 70% water","IgE; approximately 90% water"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states IgA is the most abundant immunoglobulin in human milk (constituting 90% of immunoglobulins) and that mature milk provides adequate hydration, being 87% water. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"During a single breastfeeding session, milk released 10 to 20 minutes into the feeding is called hindmilk. How does it differ from foremilk according to the lecture?","choices":["Hindmilk is released within the first 3 to 5 minutes of a feed and closely resembles skim milk.","Hindmilk contains noticeably more water and less fat content than foremilk.","Hindmilk resembles cream, reflecting its higher fat content compared to the more water-like foremilk.","Hindmilk is only present within colostrum, not within mature milk."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states hindmilk, released after 10-20 minutes into feeding, resembles cream, while foremilk (released in the first 3-5 minutes) resembles skim milk. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed in the lecture as an advantage of breastfeeding specifically for the mother?","choices":["Reduced risk of obesity later in life for the child","Improved long-term cognitive performance measured in the child","Freedom from bacterial contamination in the feeding","Accelerated uterine involution due to uterine contractions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists uterine contraction and accelerated uterine involution as advantages of breastfeeding for the mother. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which benefit of breastfeeding is listed in the lecture for the infant specifically, rather than the mother?","choices":["Modest improvements in cognitive performance and reduced risk of later obesity.","Delayed return of the mother's normal ovulatory cycles","Possible protection against maternal breast and ovarian cancer","Promotion of mother-infant bonding and greater maternal self-confidence"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists modest improvements in cognitive performance and reduced risk of obesity later in life as infant advantages of breastfeeding. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A breastfeeding mother asks how to know her infant is getting enough milk once breastfeeding is established. Which sign should reassure her, per the lecture?","choices":["Only about 1 to 2 wet diapers being produced by the infant per day","Softening of breast tissue during feeding, along with normal weight gain and lumpy, mustard-colored stools.","Chalky, pale-appearing stools rather than the expected lumpy mustard colour","A feeding routine that is still not established by around 6 weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists softening of breast tissue during feeding (indicating milk is being consumed), normal weight gain, and lumpy mustard-appearing stool as signs of a well-nourished breastfed infant. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"How many additional calories per day and how much daily protein does the lecture recommend for a breastfeeding mother?","choices":["340 kcal/day additional and 71 g/day protein","450 kcal/day additional and 27 g/day protein","500 kcal/day additional and 70 g/day protein","600 kcal/day additional and 85 g/day protein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states breastfeeding requires 500 additional kcal/day and 70 grams/day of protein. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A mother previously underwent breast reduction surgery in which the areola and nipple were completely detached and later reattached. What does the lecture indicate about her likely breastfeeding experience?","choices":["Breast surgery is said to have no effect on lactation, since milk ducts fully regenerate within a few weeks.","Implants placed below the muscle typically cause greater reductions in milk production than surgeries involving the areola.","This scenario is equivalent to breastfeeding after a total mastectomy, with capability similarly reduced.","Incisions around the areola with complete detachment of the areola and nipple are more likely to cause reductions in milk production."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that incisions around the areola and surgical techniques involving complete detachment of the areola and nipple are more likely to cause reductions in milk production. — Nutrition Pregnancy Breastfeeding 2026 Dr. McInnis.pptx, Slide 67"}]};
